                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="706-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="706-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="706-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Brauner Phanthera V</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Phanthera V is a multipattern version of the company&#8217;s fixed-cardioid Phanthera. Both are large-diaphragm FET condensers, designed &#8220;reference quality microphone which could stand beside the sound characteristics of the coveted tube models VMA and VMX.&#8221;</p><p>In other words, the Phanthera  mics are FET condensers voiced to reproduce tube-mic characteristics.</p><p>The V includes a 3-way pattern selection switch on the mic body, enabling the selection of cardioid, omnidirectional, or bidirectional (figure-of-8) patterns. Unlike the Phanthera, it also includes a -10dB pad (switched from the mic body).</p><p>The following review of the cardioid-only Phanthera is likely descriptive of the V as well:</p><blockquote><p><cite>Sound On Sound</cite></p><p>Dirk Brauner has managed to make a range of microphones that all sound larger than life in a very flattering way, and the Phanthera is no exception. Although obviously not a valve microphone, it does have something of the richness of tone and intimacy that you would expect from that technology, yet without the almost inherent noise (and heat).</p></blockquote><p></p><blockquote><p><cite>Resolution, 2008</cite><p>A much darker [sounding mic] than the Phantom, it works well with male and female vocals. Chest sounds are more pronounced, and worked close there is a definite sense of low-mid enhancement to the sound in addition to the expected proximity effect.</p><p>The original intention with the Phanthera was to ty to create a FET-based microphone that possessed many of the sonic characteristics of a valve-based design, and when I reviewed it, it was clear that this had been successful.</p></p></blockquote><p><p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Brauner/Phanthera-V">Brauner Phanthera-V</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2008.</p>
